  "Source": "D. B. McKenney and P. N. Slater, \"Design and Use of Interference Passband Filters with Wide-Angle Lenses for Multispectral Photography,\" Appl. Opt. 9, 2435-2440 (1970). doi:10.1364/AO.9.002435 - Fig. 3 caption, p. 2437 (lambda1 = 0.6580 lambda0, lambda2 = 1.5700 lambda0); structure from Fig. 2 caption",
  "Notes": "Widest low-pass:low-pass passband filter of Fig. 3, showing the long-wavelength transmission dip of single matching layers\nSpecification: Passband between the reflection bands of stacks at 0.6580 lambda0 and 1.5700 lambda0.\nReported: Fig. 3 curve c: widest passband, roughly lambda0/lambda ~0.68-1.3; text p. 2437: 'the limitation of using only single matching layers can be seen for the widest design, where the long wavelength transmission dip becomes more severe' - the dip near lambda0/lambda ~0.75 falls to roughly 0.6 (read off plot, approximate).\nNotes: Same assumptions as McKenney1970-Fig3a. QWOT at lambda0: stack 1 layers 0.658 (H/2 0.329), stack 2 layers 1.57 (H/2 0.785), M and H' 1.0. 32 layers.\nLayers from Fig. 3 caption, p. 2437 (lambda1 = 0.6580 lambda0, lambda2 = 1.5700 lambda0); structure from Fig. 2 caption; layer 1 faces the incident medium.\nNot checked: the paper reports this design only as plots.",
